# lahn lesson: an example of what a lesson can hold (pages, sections in columns, blocks)
version 2
title Meet the note F
category Examples
author lahn
description An example lesson: text with notes to click, a tip, the neck and the keys side by side, a staff to read and hear, a hidden answer, and two drills.
instrument guitar

page F, on the top line
  section wide-narrow
    block text
      text Music is written on five lines, the staff. The higher a note sits, the higher it sounds.
      text **F** sits on the top line. On the guitar it's the 1st fret of the high E string: [F4].
    block callout
      style tip
      text Press just behind the fret, not on it: the note rings clearer.
  column
    block fretboard
      frets 0 3
      dots 6:0 6:3
      lit 6:1
      caption F, lit: the high E string's 1st fret
  section
    block heading
      text Now play it
    block exercise F alone
      type notes
      notes F4
      show staff
      count 4
      pass 3
    block exercise E and F
      type notes
      notes E4 F4
      show staff
      gate no

page Where F lives
  section halves
    block heading
      text On the neck
    block fretboard
      frets 0 5
      dots 6:0 6:3:G 5:1:C
      lit 6:1
      caption Its neighbours: E open, G at the 3rd fret
  column
    block heading
      text On the keys
    block keyboard
      from C4
      to B4
      lit F4
      caption On a piano: left of the three black keys
  section
    block text
      text Read these, then listen: [E4] [F4] [G4]. Each note you click plays.
    block staff
      notes E4 F4 G4 F4 E4 F4 G4 G4
      key C major
    block reveal
      label Which note is on the top line?
      text It's [F4]: the top line of the treble staff.

page Well done
    block callout
      style remember
      text E in the top space, F on the top line, G just above the staff.
